{
  "_": [
    "Word lists for the plain-english check (prose-check.mjs sits next to this file).",
    "Edit by hand. Only put a word in 'formal' if its replacement is right in almost every context — that list is matched literally.",
    "'formal' — formal or long words with a safe plain replacement (strict finding).",
    "'hidden-verbs' — a verb hidden inside a noun; turn it back into the verb (strict finding).",
    "'empty-links' — link text that says nothing about the target (strict finding).",
    "'hype' — subjective marketing adjectives; shown only with --all (advisory).",
    "\"easy\" — words that call the reader's work easy; they carry no fact.",
    "\"loaded\" — biased or imprecise terms and their replacements.",
    "\"latin\" — abbreviations to write out in words.",
    "\"empty-openers\" — openers that take the first slot instead of the action.",
    "\"paragraph-openers\" — words a paragraph does not start with.",
    "\"hidden-negatives\" — words carrying a hidden \"not\", used to count double negatives.",
    "\"chatty\" — internet shorthand and false togetherness.",
    "\"idioms\" — sayings a reader outside your country or in translation will lose (advisory).",
    "\"let's\" is handled in prose, not here: it is fine in a statement and wrong in an instruction.",
    "\"intensifiers\" — words that strengthen an opinion and add no checkable fact (rule 4 of \"How to fix a heavy sentence\")."
  ],
